# Linux Shells and Scripting Fundamentals

A comprehensive quick-reference guide to Linux shells, command-line utilities, and the basics of shell scripting.

## 🐧 Introduction to Shells

The **shell** acts as the facilitator and interface between the user and the Operating System (OS). It interprets your text commands and passes them to the OS kernel.

* **Default Shell**: **Bash** is the default shell in most Linux distributions.
* **Check Current Shell**: Run `echo $SHELL` to see which shell you are currently using.
* **List Available Shells**: Run `cat /etc/shells` to see all installed shells on your system.
* **Switch Shells Temporarily**: Type the name of the shell (e.g., `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`) and press Enter.
* **Change Default Shell Permanently**: Use the `chsh` command (e.g., `chsh -s /usr/bin/zsh`).

